 ### Added
 
-- **Remembered OAuth consent (story persistent-consent-grants, epic oauth-session-and-consent, REQ-2026-003; migration 000057, table `oauth_consent_grants`)**: after a user has allowed an OAuth client once, the next sign-in with the same client skips the consent screen and the IdP callback issues the authorization code directly. A grant is one row per user and client (normalised scope without the per-login device id, the exact redirect URI, the client's approval time as identity binding); the screen is skipped only for an approved client, an equal redirect URI, a requested scope that is a subset of the remembered one and no `prompt=consent` (also inside `prompt=login consent`); everything else, including an unreadable table, asks again. The sign-in at the identity provider, freshness, gate l, the device-holder rule and the PKCE / state / nonce / device binding of the code are unchanged. A revocation of the client deletes the grants of every user in the same transaction (a client that is approved again does not inherit one), GDPR erasure deletes the user's grants, and users see and withdraw them on the new account page `/account/apps` ("Apps"; own grants only, existing sessions stay signed in). Migration 000057 also adds `oauth_authz_requests.force_consent` (carries `prompt=consent` to the callback). Nodes of the previous release keep asking on every login until all nodes are upgraded. ADR-033 FR1 is relaxed accordingly; see `docs/architecture/07-deployment.md`, "Remembered consent grants".
+- **Remembered OAuth consent (story persistent-consent-grants, epic oauth-session-and-consent, REQ-2026-003; migration 000057, table `oauth_consent_grants`)**: after a user has allowed an OAuth client once, the next sign-in with the same client skips the consent screen and the IdP callback issues the authorization code directly. A grant is one row per user and client (normalised scope without the per-login device id, the exact redirect URI, the client's approval time as identity binding); the screen is skipped only for an approved client, an equal redirect URI, a requested scope that is a subset of the remembered one no `prompt=consent` and no `prompt=login` (also inside a list such as `login consent`), and a device that is new or held by the same app (a device that would replace another session, for example a legacy session on the requested device id, still shows the consent screen); everything else, including an unreadable table, asks again. The sign-in at the identity provider, freshness, gate l, the device-holder rule and the PKCE / state / nonce / device binding of the code are unchanged. A revocation of the client deletes the grants of every user in the same transaction (a client that is approved again does not inherit one), GDPR erasure deletes the user's grants, and users see and withdraw them on the new account page `/account/apps` ("Apps"; own grants only, existing sessions stay signed in). Migration 000057 also adds `oauth_authz_requests.force_consent` (carries `prompt=consent` to the callback). Nodes of the previous release keep asking on every login until all nodes are upgraded; during that deploy window a `prompt=consent` / `prompt=login` request started on an old node may be skipped by a new node. The sign-in started at `/account/apps` returns there (migration 000057 widens the action CHECK of the account login requests). ADR-033 FR1 is relaxed accordingly; see `docs/architecture/07-deployment.md`, "Remembered consent grants".
 - **Admin-configurable Matrix session lifetime (story session-lifetime-setting, epic oauth-session-and-consent; migration 000056, audit action `session_lifetime_changed`)**: the section "Matrix session lifetime" on `/admin/config` sets how long a new Matrix session (OAuth and legacy login) lasts, in whole seconds from 3600 to 7776000 (1 hour to 90 days), stored as `server_config.matrix_session_ttl_seconds`. Without a saved value `NEBU_MATRIX_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS` applies, else 8 hours (unchanged default). The lifetime stays an absolute ceiling from the sign-in; a change applies to the next sign-in and existing OAuth sessions keep the ceiling they were issued with (legacy sessions follow the current setting when they are extended, lowered or raised, because they have no stored ceiling; their `expires_at` is never shortened). The form is gated like the OAuth switch (fresh sign-in, "Sign in again to save"), refuses out-of-range values next to the field, and writes one audit row with the old and new number in the same transaction. The value is read through the per-node config cache (the saving node at once, other nodes within `NEBU_OAUTH_CONFIG_CACHE_MS`) with a last-known-good rule: a failed read or a damaged row keeps the last valid saved value of the node instead of falling back to a longer env / 8 hour ceiling. A legacy login is still capped by the expiry of the identity provider's token. Migration 000056 adds the key to the `server_config` update allowlist (the second save would fail without it). See `docs/architecture/07-deployment.md`, "Matrix session lifetime".
 - **`PUT /_matrix/client/v3/rooms/{roomId}/redact/{eventId}/{txnId}` and applied `m.room.redaction` events, audit action `room_event_redacted`, migration 000051 (story state-writer-redaction; follow-ups `fw`, `fv`, `fz`, `fx` closed)**: Element's "Remove" and "Remove recent messages" work. A redaction is decided and written by the room process (own event: `events["m.room.redaction"]`; another user's event: also the `redact` level), stored with a top-level `redacts` (new column `events.redacts`, migration 000051) and served with `redacts` and `content.redacts`; the redacted event is served pruned (`content: {}`, `unsigned.redacted_because`) on every read path while its content stays in the database (Compliance Mode); a redaction of another user's event writes the audit row `room_event_redacted`. Operator query **Q-rd** (07-deployment) lists legacy unchecked redactions.
 - **`PUT /_matrix/client/v3/rooms/{roomId}/state/m.room.power_levels` (story power-levels-enforcement; follow-ups `eq`, `er`, `fr` closed)**: power levels can be changed after the room was created (Element's Roles & Permissions, `/op`, `/deop`) under the room version 10 change rules, decided and written in the room process in one transaction; new audit action `room_power_levels_changed`; the PUT is charged to the `moderation` tier; operator query Q-pl in `docs/architecture/07-deployment.md`.
